===Noun===
[[Image:Chair.png|thumb|right|A chair (item of furniture).]]
[[Image:Schienenstühle, Laschenstoß.jpg|thumb|right|Chairs (rail supports on a railway).]]
{{wikipedia|chair|chair (furniture)}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|furniture for sitting}} An item of [[furniture]] used to sit on or in, comprising a [[seat]], legs or wheels, back, and sometimes arm rests, for use by one person. Compare {{m|en|stool}}, {{m|en|couch}}, {{m|en|sofa}}, {{m|en|settee}}, {{m|en|loveseat}} and {{m|en|bench}}.
#: {{ux|en|All I need to weather a snowstorm is hot coffee, a warm fire, a good book and a comfortable '''chair'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=12|passage=There were many wooden '''chairs''' for the bulk of his visitors, and two wicker arm'''chairs''' with red cloth cushions for superior people. From the packing-cases had emerged some Indian clubs,{{nb...}}, and all these articles{{nb...}} made a scattered and untidy decoration that Mrs. Clough assiduously dusted and greatly cherished.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=19|passage=Meanwhile Nanny Broome was recovering from her initial panic and seemed anxious to make up for any kudos she might have lost, by exerting her personality to the utmost. She took the policeman's helmet and placed it on a '''chair''', and unfolded his tunic to shake it and fold it up again for him.}}
# {{senseid|en|chairperson}}{{lb|en|often with definite article, also written ''[[Chair]]''}} {{clipping of|en|chairperson}}
#: {{ux|en|Under the rules of order adopted by the board, the '''chair''' may neither make nor second motions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=March 23, 1658-9|author=[[w:Thomas Burton (politician)|Thomas Burton]]|editor=w:John Towill Rutt|title=Diary|location=London|publisher=w:Henry Colburn|year_published=1828|page=243|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eVxjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA243&lpg=PA243
|passage=The '''Chair''' behaves himself like a Busby amongst so many school-boys{{...}}and takes a little too much on him.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 5, 1887|work=The Times
|passage=It can hardly be conceived that the '''Chair''' would fail to gain the support of the House.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=March|author=Michael Robbins|title=Dr. Lardner's "Railway Economy"|journal=Railway Magazine|page=153|text=He was elected to the '''chair''' of Natural Philosophy and Astronomy in 1827 at the newly-founded London University, and became prominent in railway controversies in the 'thirties, when he came off second best in a dispute with Daniel Gooch about the effects of speed on the human frame.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 June 3|author=Lilian Greenwood talks to Paul Stephen|title=Rail's 'underlying challenges' remain|journal=Rail|page=34|text=She adds: "I'd also like to think that as '''chair''' I was friendly but firm. I wanted to encourage people to give evidence, while there are others who need to be coaxed, held to account and asked tough questions."}}
# {{senseid|en|orchestra position}} {{lb|en|music}} The seating position of a particular musician in an orchestra.
#: {{ux|en|My violin teacher used to play first '''chair''' with the Boston Pops.}}
# {{senseid|en|rail-to-sleeper hardware}} {{lb|en|rail transport}} An [[iron]] [[block]] used on [[railway]]s to support the [[rail]]s and secure them to the [[sleeper]]s, and similar devices.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1934|month=February|title=The Why and The Wherefore: Chair-keys|journal=Railway Magazine|page=139|text=The wooden or steel keys used to secure bull-head rails in their '''chairs''' are usually driven in the direction of the traffic, so that the effects of rail-creep may be made use of to wedge the keys more firmly, rather than to encourage them to drop out.}}
# {{senseid|en|conformer of cyclohexane}} {{lb|en|chemistry}} One of two possible [[conformer]]s of [[cyclohexane]] rings (the other being [[boat]]), shaped roughly like a chair.
# {{senseid|en|electric chair}} {{lb|en|informal|with ''the''}} {{ellipsis of|en|electric chair|t=device used for performing execution}}
#: {{ux|en|The court will show no mercy; if he gets convicted, it's the '''chair''' for him.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=June 1920|journal=w:The Electrical Experimenter|location=New York|page=216|column=2|passage="It was me. And I'm glad, damned glad, I didn't croak him. With this slick guy after me, it would be me for the '''chair'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Agatha Christie|title=Murder on the Orient Express|location=London|publisher=HarperCollins|year=1934|year_published= 2017|chapter=8|page=251|passage='All for a pig of a man who should have gone to the '''chair'''.'}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1949|writer=w:Isobel Lennart|title=w:Holiday Affair|role=Carl Davis|actor=Wendell Corey|passage=Believe it or not, it only looked like I was trying to send you to the '''chair'''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|director=w:Ivan Reitman|title=w:Ghostbusters II|date=16 June 1989|text="Scoleri Brothers!"  "Friends of yours?"  "I tried them for murder!  Gave them the '''chair'''!"}}
# {{senseid|en|distinguished professorship}} {{lb|en|education}} A [[distinguished]] [[professorship]] at a university.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michael Inwood|title=Martin Heidegger: the philosopher who fell for Hitler &#91;print version: Hitler's philosopher&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/books/10739165/Martin-Heidegger-the-philosopher-who-fell-for-Hitler.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Review)|location=London|date=12 April 2014|page=R11|passage=In 1928 [[w:Martin Heidegger|&#91;Martin&#93; Heidegger]] succeeded [[w:Edmund Husserl|&#91;Edmund&#93; Husserl]] to take a '''chair''' at Freiburg {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|gig as conveyance}} A [[vehicle]] for one person; either a [[sedan]] borne upon poles, or a two-wheeled carriage drawn by one horse; a [[gig]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|II|v|104|2|Enter Mortimer, brought in a '''Chayre''', and Iaylors.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1777|author=w:Richard Brinsley Sheridan|title=The School for Scandal|section=IV.iii
|passage=She always leaves her '''Chair''' at the milliner's in the next Street.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1712|author=w:Alexander Pope|chapter=w:The Rape of the Lock|title=The Beauties of Pope|location=London|publisher=G. Kearsley|year_published=1783|page=32|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cywhAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA26&lpg=PA32|passage=Think what an equipage thou haſt in air, <br> And view with ſcorn two pages and a '''chair'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=211|volume=II|passage=The '''chairs''' came at the appointed hour, and Ethel could not but be amused at the glimpses she had of the park along which they were carried; although haunted by misgivings as to the judiciousness of their destination. They were set down in a hall of large dimensions, hung round with portraits, and filled with servants, who had more the air of guards.}}
# {{senseid|en|office of authority such as bishopry}} The seat or office of a person in [[authority]], such as a [[judge]] or [[bishop]].

